Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Strength dipped across your biggest muscle groups this week — recovery is the priority right now.",
  "summary": "You put in a solid 28 sets this period with good overall tonnage, and your training structure looks reasonably balanced across muscle groups. However, the most important signal this week is that your strength index dropped in your three largest movers — chest, back, and legs — while shoulders, biceps, and triceps held flat. That pattern across multiple unrelated muscle groups at the same time is a classic sign that your body is carrying accumulated fatigue rather than underperforming on specific exercises. The good news is that this is very common during a consistent training block, and addressing it now will set you up for a strong rebound next period.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "regressing",
    "notes": "The strength index declined in chest (106 to 101), back (104 to 100), and legs (108 to 102) — three of the four main compound-lift groups. Shoulders, biceps, and triceps all held flat at 103, 102, and 104 respectively, showing no regression but also no gain. With the previous period's note pointing to steady progress, this widespread simultaneous dip is unlikely to be a technique or programming issue in isolation — it strongly suggests systemic fatigue is suppressing performance across the board. No muscle group showed a strength gain this period."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps volume sits at only 4% of total training volume (3 sets), roughly half the share of triceps at 8.4% (3 sets with higher tonnage). For a muscle-building goal, maintaining a closer balance between the elbow flexors and extensors is worth monitoring over time."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs account for 22.4% of volume split, which is the lowest of the three primary muscle groups. For a hypertrophy goal in a 28-year-old male, legs typically benefit from a higher relative volume share — consider gradually increasing leg sets in future periods."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "Strength indices dropped simultaneously across chest, back, and legs — the three largest compound movement groups — while no muscle group improved. This broad, concurrent regression is a strong indicator of accumulated systemic fatigue. A deload period of reduced volume and intensity (roughly 40–60% of normal sets and load) will allow the nervous system and muscles to recover fully, and research consistently shows performance rebounds meaningfully after a proper deload.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Deload next period by cutting your total sets to around 12–14 and reducing working weights to approximately 50–60% of your recent loads — keep the same movement patterns but treat it as active recovery, not a training stimulus.",
    "Prioritise sleep and nutrition during the deload, particularly protein intake (aim for around 1.6–2g per kg of bodyweight daily) and carbohydrate availability around sessions to support glycogen replenishment.",
    "After the deload, gradually reintroduce leg volume — consider adding one extra leg set per period over the following two to three periods to bring legs closer to parity with chest and back in your volume split.",
    "Track how your biceps tonnage compares to triceps over the next two to three periods; if the gap persists, add one additional biceps set to help balance elbow flexor and extensor development for long-term symmetry."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strength index regressed across chest, back, and legs simultaneously this period despite solid volume, strongly indicating accumulated fatigue — a deload was recommended, with a focus on recovery and rebalancing leg and biceps volume afterward."
}
